   {
     "heading": "4. Account, authentication, and cloud sync",
-    "body": "The App uses Firebase Authentication. By default, Firebase may create an anonymous app session so cloud features can operate without a separate Knowly username/password.\n\nYou may optionally sign in with Google or Apple. If you do, Firebase and Knowly may process provider identifiers, Firebase Auth UID, email address if shared, display name if shared, provider type, platform, sign-in timestamps, account-linking records, and related authentication metadata. We use this to restore progress, link devices, prevent account drift, secure the service, and prevent abuse.\n\nCloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your \"My mistakes\" history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favorites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, premium status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and Energy balances and ledgers, Spire progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features.\n\nIf you lose access to your progress, for example after reinstalling the App or choosing a different Google or Apple account, the App may offer to restore it with a one-time code. The code is sent by email, through our email provider (Resend), only to the email address already linked to your account. It expires after 10 minutes. We store only a protected (hashed) form of the code, together with attempt counters and recovery records used to prevent abuse."
+    "body": "The App uses Firebase Authentication. By default, Firebase may create an anonymous app session so cloud features can operate without a separate Knowly username/password.\n\nYou may optionally sign in with Google or Apple. If you do, Firebase and Knowly may process provider identifiers, Firebase Auth UID, email address if shared, display name if shared, provider type, platform, sign-in timestamps, account-linking records, and related authentication metadata. We use this to restore progress, link devices, prevent account drift, secure the service, and prevent abuse.\n\nCloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your \"My mistakes\" history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favorites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, premium status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and Energy balances and ledgers, Spire progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features.\n\nIf you lose access to your progress, for example after reinstalling the App or choosing a different Google or Apple account, the App may offer to restore it with a one-time code. The code is sent by email, through our email provider (Resend), only to the email address already linked to your account. It expires after 10 minutes. We store only a protected (hashed) form of the code, together with attempt counters and recovery records used to prevent abuse.\n\nIf you save a video to your favourites, we store the video ID and the time you saved it in your account so your list follows you to a new device, and we keep an aggregate count of how many accounts saved each video. The count does not show who saved a video. Your list is deleted together with your account."
   },
   {
     "heading": "5. Public, social, and historical competitive data",
-    "body": "Some data is visible to other users when you use current public or social features. This may include nickname, coin-rank avatar, profile card settings and style, the short profile-card caption you write yourself (available with a Pro profile card), streak, rank, Spire floor, steps and weekly result, weekly coins earned (shown to members of your Spire room), historical Arena and tournament results from earlier app versions, premium markers, friend activity, activity likes, community pack author information, seller notifications, and similar profile fields. Your profile-card caption is visible to other players who open your profile card; other players can report it, and we may remove captions that break our rules. If you answer the daily poll, we store your chosen option linked to your account ID to prevent repeat voting; other users only see aggregated percentages. When you save a video to your favorites, the App may show other users how many people saved that video; this is an aggregated count only and does not reveal who saved it. Visibility depends on the feature: some fields may be public to app users, some visible to friends, some visible to community pack buyers or sellers, and some visible only to moderators/admins.\n\nThe Spire groups players into weekly rooms of up to 30 places for each study language. Other app users who open the room can see your nickname, avatar and cosmetic style, your steps, place and weekly result. Some places in a room may be filled by computer-controlled participants rather than real people. Weekly room records are kept as part of the Spire history; your entry is removed from them when your account is deleted.\n\nWhen you use the Together feature with friends, those friends may see whether you studied today, how many days you both studied on the same day, the resulting friendship level and progress, Friends may also send or receive nudge signals through this feature.\n\nHistorical records created by retired features (such as the old Quiz mode, Arena matches and friend duels, tournaments, clubs, or friend gifts), including old results or participation records, may be retained only as reasonably necessary for security, fraud or abuse prevention, dispute handling, legal compliance, backup expiry, or deletion integrity. Account deletion is designed to delete or de-identify those historical records controlled by Knowly, subject only to the limited retention grounds described in this Policy. Those historical records are not presented as currently available gameplay features."
+    "body": "Some data is visible to other users when you use current public or social features. This may include nickname, coin-rank avatar, profile card settings and style, the short profile-card caption you write yourself (available with a Pro profile card), streak, rank, Spire floor, steps and weekly result, weekly coins earned (shown to members of your Spire room), historical Arena and tournament results from earlier app versions, premium markers, friend activity, activity likes, community pack author information, seller notifications, and similar profile fields. Your profile-card caption is visible to other players who open your profile card; other players can report it, and we may remove captions that break our rules. If you answer the daily poll, we store your chosen option linked to your account ID to prevent repeat voting; other users only see aggregated percentages. When you save a video to your favorites, the App may show other users how many people saved that video; this is an aggregated count only and does not reveal who saved it. Visibility depends on the feature: some fields may be public to app users, some visible to friends, some visible to community pack buyers or sellers, and some visible only to moderators/admins.\n\nThe Spire groups players into weekly rooms of up to 30 places for each study language. Other app users who open the room can see your nickname, avatar and cosmetic style, your steps, place and weekly result. Some places in a room may be filled by computer-controlled participants rather than real people. Weekly room records are kept as part of the Spire history; your entry is removed from them when your account is deleted.\n\nWhen you use the Together feature with friends, those friends may see whether you studied today, how many days you both studied on the same day, the resulting friendship level and progress, Friends may also send or receive nudge signals through this feature.\n\nHistorical records created by retired features (such as the old Quiz mode, Arena matches and friend duels, tournaments, clubs, friend gifts, or the old ideas board (submitted ideas, likes and reports on ideas)), including old results or participation records, may be retained only as reasonably necessary for security, fraud or abuse prevention, dispute handling, legal compliance, backup expiry, or deletion integrity. Account deletion is designed to delete or de-identify those historical records controlled by Knowly, subject only to the limited retention grounds described in this Policy. Those historical records are not presented as currently available gameplay features."
   },
   {
     "heading": "6. User content, community packs, reports, and moderation",
-    "body": "If you create Community Packs, submit flashcard text, translations, reports, profile names, survey answers, ratings/votes, or other user-provided content, we process the content you provide and related metadata. This may include titles, descriptions, card text and translations, normalized text used for moderation, report reasons, reporter/reported user IDs and names, author IDs, buyer/seller IDs, moderation decisions, timestamps, platform, app version, audit fields, ranking scores, vote counts, report counts, and related safety signals.\n\nPublished Community Packs and approved catalog metadata may be visible in the App. Pack cards may be visible to the author, moderators, and users who access or purchase the pack with coins.\n\nIf you comment on a Community Pack you have added, we store your comment text, your author ID and display name, the pack ID, timestamps, reaction counts, and pin status. Comments are visible to other users who have added the same pack, remain attached to the pack, and are not private messages. The pack's author can pin one comment and hide a comment from the pack's comment list for other viewers; a hidden comment is not deleted and may still be reviewed if reported. You can delete your own comment at any time, and any user can report a specific comment (separately from reporting the whole pack) using the same report categories described above. If someone comments on a Community Pack you authored, we send you an in-app notification containing a short excerpt of the comment so you can review it.\n\nA Community Pack's author can edit the pack's title, description, and cards after it is published. Edited content replaces the previous version immediately and does not go through a separate moderation review before becoming visible to other users; we keep the previous version and edit metadata for audit and abuse-investigation purposes.\n\nTo check Community Packs for mistakes and to show them in other interface languages, the text of a pack (title, description, and card text) may be sent to our AI provider (OpenAI) for review and automatic translation. Translated versions may be shown to other users who view the pack. When we reply to a report you sent, a moderator may use AI assistance to draft the reply from the report text; a person reviews the reply before it is sent to you in the App.\n\nWe may review, block, remove, hide, de-identify, retain, or disclose user content, comments, and reports when needed to operate moderation, enforce the Terms, protect users, investigate abuse, handle disputes, prevent fraud, comply with law, respond to copyright or rights complaints, or preserve service integrity."
+    "body": "If you create Community Packs, submit flashcard text, translations, reports, profile names, survey answers, ratings/votes, or other user-provided content, we process the content you provide and related metadata. This may include titles, descriptions, card text and translations, normalized text used for moderation, report reasons, reporter/reported user IDs and names, author IDs, buyer/seller IDs, moderation decisions, timestamps, platform, app version, audit fields, ranking scores, vote counts, report counts, and related safety signals.\n\nPublished Community Packs and approved catalog metadata may be visible in the App. Pack cards may be visible to the author, moderators, and users who access or purchase the pack with coins.\n\nIf you comment on a Community Pack you have added, we store your comment text, your author ID and display name, the pack ID, timestamps, reaction counts, and pin status. Comments are visible to other users who have added the same pack, remain attached to the pack, and are not private messages. The pack's author can pin one comment and hide a comment from the pack's comment list for other viewers; a hidden comment is not deleted and may still be reviewed if reported. You can delete your own comment at any time, and any user can report a specific comment (separately from reporting the whole pack) using the same report categories described above. If someone comments on a Community Pack you authored, we send you an in-app notification containing a short excerpt of the comment so you can review it.\n\nA Community Pack's author can edit the pack's title, description, and cards after it is published. Edited content replaces the previous version immediately and does not go through a separate moderation review before becoming visible to other users; we keep the previous version and edit metadata for audit and abuse-investigation purposes.\n\nTo check Community Packs for mistakes and to show them in other interface languages, the text of a pack (title, description, and card text) may be sent to our AI provider (OpenAI) for review and automatic translation. Translated versions may be shown to other users who view the pack. When we reply to a report you sent, a moderator may use AI assistance to draft the reply from the report text; a person reviews the reply before it is sent to you in the App.\n\nWhen a Community Pack is approved for publication, the text of its cards in the language being learned (up to 300 characters per card) may be sent from our servers to our AI provider (OpenAI) to generate spoken audio for the cards. Only the card text is sent; no account details, names, or identifiers are sent with it. The generated audio files are stored in our cloud storage (Google Firebase) and played to users who open the pack. Because a clip depends only on its text, the same clip may be reused for identical card text in other packs. Audio is kept for as long as it is needed to provide the packs that use it and may be removed at any time.\n\nWe may review, block, remove, hide, de-identify, retain, or disclose user content, comments, and reports when needed to operate moderation, enforce the Terms, protect users, investigate abuse, handle disputes, prevent fraud, comply with law, respond to copyright or rights complaints, or preserve service integrity."
